WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.014 --> 00:00:02.880
INTRO:
When people think of the highest paying jobs in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:08.400
the world, they generally think of top executives.&nbsp;
And this makes sense given that people like Sundar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:14.480
Pichai and Tim Cook often pull in over a hundred&nbsp;
million dollars per year. But, there’s actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:20.880
one job that pays even higher and that’s being a&nbsp;
hedge fund manager. The top hedge fund managers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:27.120
aren’t just billionaires, but they literally&nbsp;
make billions every single year. In 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:34.240
the top paid hedge fund manager was ironically Ken&nbsp;
Griffin from Citadel who pulled in a total of $2.5&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:41.040
billion. Considering this insane income, you would&nbsp;
think that these guys are the next Warren Buffett,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:47.200
but this isn’t exactly true. At least with top&nbsp;
executives, they generally create a lot more value&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:52.480
for shareholders than they’re paid themselves.&nbsp;
For example, since Tim Cook became CEO,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:59.120
Apple’s market cap has grown over $2 trillion.&nbsp;
So, it’s understandable that Tim Cook is paid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:04.560
so much. But, with hedge fund managers, their&nbsp;
performance is generally not only mediocre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:10.800
but they often don’t even beat the market. Ken&nbsp;
Griffin's Citadel, for example, grew a solid 26%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:20.320
in 2021. This sounds really good until you realize&nbsp;
that the S&amp;P 500 itself grew 26.89% in 2021. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:26.080
Ken basically only had average performance, yet&nbsp;
he received the highest salary in the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:32.960
world. So, why are hedge fund managers paid so&nbsp;
much when their performance is at best mediocre.

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:37.840
CLIENT BASE:&nbsp;

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:42.880
To understand why hedge fund managers are paid&nbsp;
so much, we have to first understand what type of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:48.320
person even invests in a hedge fund in the first&nbsp;
place. In terms of qualifications, generally, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:53.200
have to be an accredited investor and you have to&nbsp;
be willing to invest a minimum amount of capital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:54.793
to be part of a given hedge fund. The top hedge&nbsp;
funds generally have even stricter requirements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.793 --> 00:01:58.640
given their notoriety. Citadel, for example,&nbsp;
requires that you have $10 million in investable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:04.320
assets to join their funds. Considering this, the&nbsp;
average investor in hedge funds are billionaires&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:09.840
and institutional investors like banks, pension&nbsp;
funds, and retirement funds. And often, the top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:15.920
priority for these guys is not capital growth&nbsp;
but rather risk minimization. Here’s the thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:20.480
if you’re a billionaire, your company and/or&nbsp;
investments have been demolishing the market for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:26.320
several years if not decades. Take Jeff Bezos,&nbsp;
for example. Since Amazon went public, their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:31.760
stock has grown from $2 a share to about $3000&nbsp;
a share today after accounting for stock splits.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:38.480
If we annualize this return, we get an&nbsp;
average annual return of over 35%. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:45.200
that might now sound like a lot given that&nbsp;
the S&amp;P 500 already averages 7-8% per year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:50.560
But, when you compound this growth, things&nbsp;
get wacky extremely quickly. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:57.840
if you average 8% per year for 25 years, you end&nbsp;
up with a solid 7.34x return. But, if you average&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:05.280
35% per year for 25 years, you end up with a 5500x&nbsp;
return. Considering this, someone like Jeff Bezos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:10.400
isn’t trying to maximize returns when they invest&nbsp;
in a hedge fund. They’re trying to minimize risk.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:14.160
This becomes even more important when you&nbsp;
consider that most billionaires have their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:20.400
entire net worth tied up with 1 maybe 2 companies.&nbsp;
But wait a minute, how exactly does a hedge fund&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:25.680
even minimize risk in the first place? Well, one&nbsp;
of their risk mitigation strategies is in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:31.040
name, hedging. If you’re not familiar with what&nbsp;
hedging is, it’s basically buying insurance on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:36.400
all of your positions by also betting the opposite&nbsp;
of what you think is going to happen. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:41.040
let’s say I’m a massive fan of Tesla and I think&nbsp;
it’s going to 10x from where it’s at right now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:47.680
So, I go ahead and invest $10 into Tesla. If I&nbsp;
wanted to hedge this position, I would spend an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:53.360
additional $1 on betting that Tesla will go down.&nbsp;
There’s multiple ways to do this from shorting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:58.880
to buying options to performing equity swaps, but&nbsp;
the specifics don’t really matter. All you need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:05.680
know is that this $1 position goes up in value if&nbsp;
Tesla stock goes down. Now, if my initial thesis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:11.840
was right and Tesla does 10X, I’ll lose the $1&nbsp;
that I bet against Tesla, but my initial $10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:17.680
investment would grow to $100 meaning that&nbsp;
I still profited $89. On the other hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:23.760
if my thesis is wrong and Tesla halves in value,&nbsp;
my initial investment would go down to $5. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:28.880
the $1 that I bet against Tesla might’ve grown&nbsp;
to $3 or $4 meaning that I’m left with a total&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:35.200
of $8 or $9. In this case, I only lost 20-30%&nbsp;
of my initial investment while the average Tesla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:41.840
investor lost the full 50%. And this is really the&nbsp;
core of what hedge fund managers are paid to do.

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:45.280
BETA:&nbsp;

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:50.400
Every hedge fund has a beta value depending on&nbsp;
how much the given fund hedges their positions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:55.680
If a fund has a beta value of 1, that means that&nbsp;
their positions will perform exactly in line with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:03.120
the general market. If the S&amp;P 500 goes up 5%, the&nbsp;
hedge fund will also make 5% and vice versa. If a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:08.880
fund has a beta value over 1, this means that the&nbsp;
fund is more volatile than the general market. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:15.920
the market grows 5%, such a fund may grow 10%, but&nbsp;
the thing to note is that, if the market drops 5%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:21.200
such a fund will lose 10%. While there are&nbsp;
some funds out there with a beta over 1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:26.800
this is by no means the average hedge fund. A fund&nbsp;
could also have a negative beta which means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:31.680
the fund is inversely correlated to the market.&nbsp;
But, given that markets trend up with time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:36.880
it usually makes no sense to invest in such a fund&nbsp;
for an extended period of time. The average hedge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:42.960
fund usually has a beta between 0 and 1. This&nbsp;
means that the fund is correlated to the market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:48.240
but it will usually underperform the market both&nbsp;
to the upside and the downside. And with that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:53.600
being said, take a look at this graph comparing&nbsp;
S&amp;P 500 and hedge fund performance. Clearly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.600 --> 00:06:00.000
hedge funds basically never beat the S&amp;P 500, but&nbsp;
focus on what happens when the S&amp;P 500 is posting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:08.480
losses. Between 2000 and 2002, the S&amp;P 500 lost&nbsp;
43% due to the dotcom crash. Meanwhile, hedge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:15.360
funds didn’t even have a single negative year.&nbsp;
Similarly, in 2008, the S&amp;P 500 lost 37% while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:21.680
hedge funds only lost 22%. Now, for most people,&nbsp;
it would’ve just been better to hold the S&amp;P 500&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:27.360
through such crashes given its superior overall&nbsp;
returns. But, this doesn't apply to everyone. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:34.720
example, let’s go back to Jeff Bezos. During the&nbsp;
dotcom crash, Amazon stock crashed 95%. And given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:40.480
that 99% of his wealth was tied up with Amazon,&nbsp;
he was bagholding quite a bit of Amazon stock.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:44.880
Considering this, would it really have made sense&nbsp;
for Jeff to invest the cash that he did have into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:50.880
the S&amp;P 500 and bag hold that as well? Not really.&nbsp;
It would’ve made a lot more sense for him to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:56.160
invested that money in a hedge fund and settled&nbsp;
for lower gains and more safety. So clearly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.160 --> 00:07:00.480
hedge funds don’t have to beat the market for&nbsp;
them to be appealing in certain situations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:05.440
With that being said, it really makes no sense&nbsp;
to even compare hedge fund returns with the S&amp;P&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:10.880
500. That’s like comparing bonds with the S&amp;P&nbsp;
500 and then concluding that bonds suck given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:16.240
that they have lower yields. It’s not that&nbsp;
one is superior and the other is inferior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:19.520
they’re simply different instruments&nbsp;
that make sense in different situations.

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:23.600
ALPHA:&nbsp;

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:27.840
If it doesn’t make sense to compare hedge fund&nbsp;
performance with the S&amp;P 500 though, how should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:34.080
hedge funds be evaluated? Well, the answer&nbsp;
is alpha. Every investment has a risk profile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:39.760
which like hedge funds are described by beta. And&nbsp;
alpha describes how good your investment did in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:45.840
relation to its beta value. In other words, alpha&nbsp;
is simply a way to measure risk adjusted returns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:51.760
For example, let’s say that in a given year,&nbsp;
the S&amp;P 500 returned 12%. Meanwhile, you’re a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:57.680
fund manager who decides to invest in Apple. You&nbsp;
think that Apple will outperform the S&amp;P 500,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:04.240
but Apple is a bit riskier with a beta value of&nbsp;
1.2. Now, let’s say that Apple ends up producing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:10.560
return of 15% which is 3% higher than the S&amp;P 500.&nbsp;
By using this equation, we can determine that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:16.880
alpha of this investment was positive 1.2% meaning&nbsp;
that it was a good investment. In this example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:21.440
Apple stock outperformed the S&amp;P&nbsp;
500 and our alpha was positive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:27.760
But, an investment doesn’t need to outperform the&nbsp;
S&amp;P 500 for our alpha to be positive. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:33.120
instead of investing in Apple stock, let’s say&nbsp;
we buy an Apple bond that yields 5% per year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:36.800
While this investment has a much&nbsp;
lower return than the S&amp;P 500,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:42.560
it’s also much safer. And if its beta value is&nbsp;
low enough, the Apple bond could still produce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:47.600
a positive alpha. Something to keep in mind&nbsp;
though is that this same logic also works the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:52.800
other way around. Just because you beat the market&nbsp;
doesn't mean that you’ll have a positive alpha.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:58.880
For example, if you produce a 100% return but&nbsp;
your beta is 10, your alpha won’t be looking that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:04.000
great. Alpha is the primary benchmark institutions&nbsp;
and billionaires look at when they invest in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:09.760
hedge fund. And boasting a high alpha is usually&nbsp;
the number 1 goal of any hedge fund. Ray Dalio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:14.480
for example, has an entire group of funds&nbsp;
that are called Pure Alpha. So, next time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:18.800
you see a headline that’s trashing on hedge fund&nbsp;
returns, don’t be fooled into thinking that these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:24.080
funds are garbage. The only way to really tell if&nbsp;
they’re garbage or not is to look at their alpha.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:28.000
COMPENSATION:&nbsp;

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:32.320
Now that we’ve discussed the true goals of hedge&nbsp;
fund managers and how exactly they achieve these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:37.440
goals, let’s talk about their compensation. The&nbsp;
standard fee structure amongst hedge funds is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:42.960
2 and 20 rule. This means that fund managers&nbsp;
are paid 2% of the entire fund's assets under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:48.000
management every single year regardless of how&nbsp;
the fund performs. On top of this, they receive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:53.920
20% of any profit they generate. This means that&nbsp;
such funds have to grow over 2% every single year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.920 --> 00:09:59.040
just for the clients to breakeven. Fortunately,&nbsp;
virtually all hedge funds average a return of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:04.480
at least 2% per year, but this does heavily&nbsp;
dampen the net returns that the clients make.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:08.640
Not to mention, many of the positions that&nbsp;
hedge funds take are short term positions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:12.000
meaning that clients have to pay short&nbsp;
term capital gains taxes on these returns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:17.120
Some funds do offer lower fee structures, but&nbsp;
basically all of the flagship funds you hear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:21.840
about on the news use this structure. And&nbsp;
given that such funds generally manage over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:26.320
a hundred billion dollars, it’s not surprising&nbsp;
that their fund managers walk away with billions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:31.440
every year. In terms of the client’s perspective,&nbsp;
while it is true that these fees are quite high,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:36.160
you can’t really find hedging services for&nbsp;
cheaper. You may be able to find better returns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:41.040
for cheaper, but you won't be able to find hedging&nbsp;
services for cheaper given that hedging is quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:48.400
work intensive. Analysts on wall street generally&nbsp;
work 100 to 120 hours every single week. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.400 --> 00:10:53.120
this is quite brutal, especially in the early&nbsp;
days when you’re not making that much money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:59.120
if you stick with it for 5-10 years, it’s quite&nbsp;
reasonable to earn 7 figures every year. So, if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:03.600
you’re looking to make 7 figures when you’re young&nbsp;
and you don’t want to start a business, pursuing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:08.720
wall street is likely the most straightforward&nbsp;
way to achieve it. With that being said though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:14.640
there is a dark side to hedge funds. In order to&nbsp;
achieve high alpha and minimize beta, hedge funds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:19.840
often use every trick you can think of. Everything&nbsp;
from market manipulation and insider trading to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:25.680
pump and dumps and securities fraud. Most of the&nbsp;
time, the SEC won't do anything about it, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:29.848
even if they do, for many hedge funds, it’s just&nbsp;
the cost of doing business. The perks of insider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.848 --> 00:11:30.640
trading and market manipulation are simply way&nbsp;
higher than the minuscule fines from the SEC. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:36.560
in the end, it just comes down to one question: do&nbsp;
the ends justify the means. Wall street tends to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.560 --> 00:11:42.080
think that it does while retail traders think that&nbsp;
it doesn’t. Which side are you guys on? Comment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.080 --> 00:11:47.120
that down below. Also, drop a like if you think&nbsp;
wall street needs to be held more accountable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

